## Binary Search

A binary search is a search algorithm that works by repeatedly dividing the search space in half. It compares the middle element of the search space to the target value. If the target value is less than the middle element, the search space is reduced to the lower half. If the target value is greater than the middle element, the search space is reduced to the upper half. This process continues until the target value is found or the search space is empty.

The following code implements a binary search:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int binary_search(int *id_sequence, int size, int target) {
    int low = 0;
    int high = size - 1;
    int mid;

    while (low <= high) {
        mid = (low + high) / 2;

        if (id_sequence[mid] == target) {
            return mid;
        } else if (id_sequence[mid] < target) {
            low = mid + 1;
        } else {
            high = mid - 1;
        }
    }

    return -1;
}

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    int id_sequence[3] = {1, 5, 7};
    int i;

    // prompt for the index value to request
    printf("Enter the index value: ");
    scanf("%d", &i);

    // if it is in bounds, return the value at the index in id_sequence
    if (i >= 0 && i < 3) {
        printf("The value at index %d is %d\n", i, id_sequence[i]);
    } else {
        // otherwise, use a binary search to find the index value
        int index = binary_search(id_sequence, 3, i);

        if (index == -1) {
            printf("The index value %d is out of bounds\n", i);
        } else {
            printf("The value at index %d is %d\n", index, id_sequence[index]);
        }
    }
}
```

