## Memory Management in C++

In C++, memory management is handled by the `new` and `delete` operators. The `new` operator allocates memory and returns a pointer to the allocated memory. The `delete` operator frees the memory pointed to by a pointer.

Here is an example of a C++ program that uses the `new` and `delete` operators to allocate and free memory:

```cpp
#include <iostream>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    // allocate a large buffer and copy in 100 characters from stdin
    char* buf = new char[1024];
    int i = 0;
    while ((i < 1024) && (buf[i] = getchar())!= EOF) {
        i++;
    }
    buf[i] = '\0';

    // print the buffer
    std::cout << buf << std::endl;

    // free the buffer
    delete[] buf;

    return 0;
}
```

The program allocates a large buffer and copies in 100 characters from stdin. It then prints the buffer and frees the buffer.

The program is a bit contrived, but it demonstrates the basic principles of memory management in C++.

