## Reading from a file

To read from a file, we can use the `fscanf()` function.

The `fscanf()` function takes two arguments: a file pointer and a format string. The format string is a string that describes how the data should be read from the file.

The format string can contain the following characters:

- `%f` - read a float
- `%d` - read an integer
- `%s` - read a string

The format string can also contain the following modifiers:

- `*` - read the next value from the file, but don't store it
- `n` - store the number of characters read into the variable specified by `n`

Here's an example of reading from a file:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <limits.h>

#define FILENAME "data.csv"

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    // open file
    FILE *fp = fopen(FILENAME, "r");
    if (fp == NULL) {
        printf("Error opening file\n");
        return 1;
    }

    // read from file
    float a;
    float b;
    float c;
    fscanf(fp, "%f,%f,%f", &a, &b, &c);

    // close file
    fclose(fp);

    return 0;
}
```

