## 2.2.2. File descriptors

A file descriptor is a unique identifier for a file. It is used to access the file in the operating system.

The following table lists some of the most common file descriptors:

| File descriptor | Description |
| --------------- | ----------- |
| 0               | Standard input |
| 1               | Standard output |
| 2               | Standard error |

To get the file descriptor for a file, use the `open()` function. The `open()` function takes three arguments:

- The name of the file
- The mode in which the file is opened
- The permissions of the file

The mode argument can be one of the following:

- `O_RDONLY` - Open the file for reading only
- `O_WRONLY` - Open the file for writing only
- `O_RDWR` - Open the file for both reading and writing
- `O_CREAT` - Create the file if it does not exist
- `O_APPEND` - Append data to the end of the file

The permissions argument is used to set the permissions of the file. It is a bitmask that can be used to set the permissions for the owner, the group, and others.

The following table lists some of the most common permissions:

| Permission | Description |
| ---------- | ----------- |
| 0644       | Read and write for owner and read for others |
| 0600       | Read and write for owner only |
| 0400       | Read for owner only |

To close a file descriptor, use the `close()` function.

The following example shows how to use the `open()`, `write()`, `read()`, and `close()` functions to read and write to a file:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <fcntl.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) { 
    char* important_config = "important_config";

    // save the important config to a file
	int fd = open(important_config, O_CREAT | O_WRONLY, 0644);
	if (fd == -1) {
		perror("open");
		exit(1);
	}
	write(fd, "important config", 16);
	close(fd);

	// read the important config from the file
	fd = open(important_config, O_RDONLY);
	if (fd == -1) {
		perror("open");
		exit(1);
	}
	char buf[16];
	read(fd, buf, 16);
	close(fd);

	// print the important config
	printf("important config: %s\n", buf);

	return 0;
}
