### 4.2.1.1. The `for` Loop

The `for` loop is a common way to iterate over a collection of items. It is a control flow statement that executes a block of code a specified number of times.

The syntax for the `for` loop is:

```c
for (initialization; condition; increment) {
    // code to execute
}
```

The `initialization` statement is executed once before the loop starts. The `condition` statement is evaluated before each iteration. If the condition is true, the code in the body of the loop is executed. If the condition is false, the loop terminates. The `increment` statement is executed after each iteration.

The following example shows how to use a `for` loop to iterate over an array of vehicles:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    char *vehicles[] = {"boat", "car", "truck", "train"};

    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
        printf("%s\n", vehicles[i]);
    }
}
```

The `for` loop in this example initializes a counter variable, `i`, to zero. The condition statement checks whether `i` is less than four. If it is, the code in the body of the loop is executed. The increment statement increments `i` by one after each iteration.

### 4.2.1.2. The `while` Loop

The `while` loop is another common way to iterate over a collection of items. It is a control flow statement that executes a block of code while a specified condition is true.

The syntax for the `while` loop is:

```c
while (condition) {
    // code to execute
}
```

The `condition` statement is evaluated before each iteration. If the condition is true, the code in the body of the loop is executed. If the condition is false, the loop terminates.

The following example shows how to use a `while` loop to iterate over an array of vehicles:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    char *vehicles[] = {"boat", "car", "truck", "train"};

    int i = 0;
    while (i < 4) {
        printf("%s\n", vehicles[i]);
        i++;
    }
}
```

The `while` loop in this example initializes a counter variable, `i`, to zero. The condition statement checks whether `i` is less than four. If it is, the code in the body of the loop is executed. The increment statement increments `i` by one after each iteration.

