}
```

In this example, the `for` loop iterates over the numbers from 0 to 4, and prints "Hello, world!" to the console.

### 2.2.2.2. The `while` Loop

The `while` loop is another common way to iterate over a collection of items. It is used to repeat a block of code while a condition is true.

```c
int i = 0;
while (i < 5) {
    printf("Hello, world!\n");
    i++;
}
```

In this example, the `while` loop iterates over the numbers from 0 to 4, and prints "Hello, world!" to the console. The `i++` statement is used to increment the value of `i` by 1 each time the loop is executed.

### 2.2.2.3. The `do-while` Loop

The `do-while` loop is a variation of the `while` loop that is used to repeat a block of code at least once.

```c
int i = 0;
do {
    printf("Hello, world!\n");
    i++;
} while (i < 5);
```

In this example, the `do-while` loop iterates over the numbers from 0 to 4, and prints "Hello, world!" to the console. The `do-while` loop will execute the block of code at least once, even if the condition is false the first time.

